    "slug": "make-it-stick-review",
    "title": "Make It Stick Review: Study Science for Students",
    "excerpt": "Honest Make It Stick review: what the learning-science classic says about retrieval, spacing and interleaving, who it suits, and how to use it for revision.",
    "body": "**Buy the book:** [SHOPEE_AFFILIATE_LINK: Make It Stick: The Science of Successful Learning]\n\n*Make It Stick* is one of the most practical books on how learning really works. It explains why rereading and highlighting feel productive but fade fast, and why self-testing, spacing out review and mixing topics build longer-lasting knowledge. It's best for IGCSE, A-Level and Pre-University students, and for the parents and teachers supporting them.\n\n### What the book is\n\n*Make It Stick: The Science of Successful Learning* was published in 2014 by the Belknap Press of Harvard University Press. It's a collaboration between two cognitive scientists, Henry L. Roediger III and Mark A. McDaniel of Washington University in St. Louis, and Peter C. Brown, a writer and former management consultant. The scientists bring the research and Brown brings the storytelling. That mix is why the book reads more like a series of case studies than a textbook.\n\nThe central argument is simple and a little uncomfortable. Many of the study habits students rely on most, like rereading notes, underlining, highlighting and cramming the night before, create what the authors call an illusion of mastery. The material feels familiar, so we assume we know it. But familiarity isn't the same as being able to recall and use knowledge under exam conditions, and those gains fade quickly.\n\n### Who it's for, by level\n\n**IGCSE, A-Level and Pre-University students** will get the most from it. These are the levels where revision loads are heavy, exams test recall across many topics, and changing *how* you study pays off quickly. A motivated IGCSE student can read it independently. A-Level and Pre-University students will appreciate the reasoning behind each technique.\n\n**Degree and Professional learners** will find it just as relevant. The book draws examples from fields such as medicine and aviation as well as classrooms, and its ideas transfer to any course with a lot to remember.\n\n**Parents of Primary and Lower Secondary children** won't hand this book to their child, but they'll find it useful for shaping homework routines, such as quizzing instead of re-reading together.\n\n**Teachers** at any level will find ideas they can bring into lessons, especially low-stakes quizzing.\n\n### Key takeaways\n\n#### 1. Retrieval practice beats rereading\nThe book's most important idea is that pulling information *out* of your memory strengthens it far more than putting it back in by re-reading. Flashcards, practice questions and trying to write down everything you remember about a topic all count. The effort of recalling is the point.\n\n#### 2. Space it out\nCramming can get you through tomorrow's test, but it doesn't last. The authors explain why returning to material after a gap, once a little forgetting has set in, produces more durable learning. That means a revision plan that comes back to each topic several times, rather than covering it once.\n\n#### 3. Mix it up (interleaving)\nPractising one type of problem over and over feels efficient. The book argues that mixing different topics or problem types in one session is harder in the moment but builds better judgement. You learn to recognise *which* method a question needs, which is exactly what exam papers demand.\n\n#### 4. Difficulty can be desirable\nA recurring theme is that some struggle is a sign learning is happening, not that it's failing. Trying to answer a question before you've been taught the method, or explaining an idea in your own words, takes more effort and pays back more.\n\n#### 5. Know what you don't know\nThe authors spend time on calibration: checking your sense of what you know against real evidence, such as a quiz score. Students are often overconfident about topics they've only re-read, and testing yourself is the cure.\n\n#### 6. Be cautious about \"learning styles\"\nThe book challenges the popular idea that teaching should be matched to a person's preferred learning style. The authors argue that what matters more is matching the method to the material and using active techniques such as retrieval.\n\n### Limitations and who it's not for\n\n*Make It Stick* isn't a quick read. It explains ideas through extended stories, and some readers will wish it got to the practical tips faster. Students who want a one-page checklist may find the narrative style slow, so a summary of its techniques can be a good starting point before reading the full book.\n\nIt's also written for an adult and older-teen audience. It isn't a book for Primary or Lower Secondary children to read themselves. And while it's strong on *why* techniques work, it leaves you to build your own revision schedule. It won't plan your IGCSE subjects for you.\n\nFinally, the techniques feel harder than the habits they replace. Self-testing shows you your gaps in a way re-reading never does. That's the point, but it takes some resilience to stick with.\n\n### How to pair Make It Stick with ET AI on Poket School\n\nThe book tells you *what* to do. These Poket School features make those techniques easier to practise every day.\n\n- **Retrieval practice with ET AI Studio.** Upload your notes or a photo of a textbook page, and ET AI Studio can turn it into quizzes and other study materials. Use the quizzes to test yourself *before* you re-read, not after.\n- **Spacing with Exam Prep.** Exam Prep turns notes you add into a day-by-day plan that ends on your exam date, with lessons, quizzes and a full mock. That gives you a built-in structure to return to topics over time instead of cramming.\n- **Desirable difficulty with ET Maths.** Snap or type a problem and ET Maths can walk you through it step by step, or show a full worked solution. Try the problem yourself first, then use step-by-step mode to check your thinking. Save the full solution for last.\n- **Interleaving with your ET AI teachers.** Mix questions from different topics in one session with your subject teacher, such as ET Biology, ET Chemistry or ET Physics, rather than drilling a single chapter.\n- **Calibration with the Bloom's mastery tracker.** Poket School's Bloom's tracker shows how deeply you understand each topic across six cognitive levels. That's a useful reality check against the illusion of mastery the book warns about.\n- **Planning help.** ET Exam Coach covers exam technique, question decoding and revision planning across subjects, and ET Study Coach helps with goals, daily plans and habits.\n\nA note on formats: ET AI Studio offers lessons, quizzes, mind maps, audio and more. In the spirit of *Make It Stick*, use that variety to keep testing yourself in different ways rather than sticking to one \"style\".\n\n### Verdict\n\n*Make It Stick* is one of the most useful books an IGCSE, A-Level or Pre-University student can read before revision starts, and it's valuable for parents and teachers too. Its message is backed by research and easy to act on: test yourself, space your review, mix your practice and welcome the struggle. The storytelling style won't suit everyone, and you'll need to build your own plan around it. But few study books change *how* you work as much as this one.\n\n**Best for:** IGCSE to Degree students, parents and teachers. **Skip if:** you want a short checklist rather than a book.\n\n**Get your copy:** [SHOPEE_AFFILIATE_LINK: Make It Stick: The Science of Successful Learning]\n\n---",
